"""Ascend fused-list executor for the paired ``_foreach_*`` binary operators.

``_foreach_{sub,div,pow}.List`` pair two tensor lists position by position (the
in-place forms write back into the first).  The generic executor stages both
lists in a metadata table and addresses every element through a pointer read
back from it, which on this CANN build costs one narrow transaction per element
and dominates the call even at 256x256.

Here a run of consecutive homogeneous entries -- same dtype, same shape and
element count, all contiguous -- is executed by *one* launch whose programs each
walk every entry's tile in order (grid ``(blocks,)``).  Keeping the entry
pointers as static kernel arguments, rather than a pointer table or a chain of
per-slot branches, is what lets the backend emit block-wide DMA transfers: a
per-slot ``if`` chain measured 4-5x slower than this serial walk, and a pointer
read out of a metadata buffer loses the alignment information the wide transfer
needs.

Entries that are non-contiguous or broadcast are indexed through a stride
decomposition in a per-entry kernel.

Tile sizing
-----------
The unified buffer is 192 KiB and every lane costs four bytes in it (this
backend promotes 16-bit arithmetic to fp32 in the vector unit), so a three-array
tile -- two inputs and the result -- tops out at 16384 elements whatever the
storage width.  Measured on the target, the tile width is the single largest
lever on the 16-bit path: at 512x512 a 1024-element tile ran 2.9x slower than an
8192-element one, and at 1024x1024 the throughput kept rising until the buffer
limit.  ``_plan_block`` therefore sizes the tile from the element count instead
of pinning it per dtype.

The in-place forms (``zs[i] is xs[i]``) get their own two-array kernel: the
result overwrites the first operand, so the launch moves two streams instead of
three (measured 10% at 1024x1024 fp16, 17% at fp32).
"""

